# HTTP — How the Web Works

When you open a website, your browser is constantly communicating with servers.

You type:

```text
https://example.com
```

Your browser sends a **request** to a server.

The server processes that request and sends back a **response**.

This communication is mainly handled using **HTTP**.

---

# What is HTTP?

**HTTP** stands for:

> **HyperText Transfer Protocol**

It is a protocol used for communication between a **client** and a **server**.

The client is usually your browser.

```text
Browser (Client)
       |
       | HTTP Request
       ↓
     Server
       |
       | HTTP Response
       ↓
Browser (Client)
```

HTTP defines how this communication should happen.

---

# Client and Server

### Client

The client is the device or application requesting something.

Examples:

* Chrome
* Safari
* Firefox
* Mobile applications
* JavaScript applications

### Server

The server receives requests and sends responses.

It may:

* Process data
* Access a database
* Authenticate users
* Return HTML
* Return JSON
* Handle business logic

---

# A Simple Example

When you visit:

```text
https://example.com
```

Your browser might send a request like:

```http
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
```

The server processes it and sends something back.

For example:

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html
```

followed by the webpage content.

---

# HTTP Request

An HTTP request is a message sent from the client to the server.

A request generally contains:

```text
Request
├── Method
├── URL
├── Headers
└── Body
```

---

# 1. HTTP Methods

HTTP methods tell the server **what we want to do**.

The most common methods are:

| Method | Purpose               |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| GET    | Retrieve data         |
| POST   | Send/create data      |
| PUT    | Replace/update data   |
| PATCH  | Partially update data |
| DELETE | Delete data           |

---

# GET

`GET` is used to retrieve data.

Example:

```http
GET /users
```

This basically means:

> "Give me the users."

A browser request for a webpage is also commonly a GET request.

---

# POST

`POST` is generally used to send data to the server.

Example:

```http
POST /users
```

The request might contain:

```json
{
    "name": "Nikitha",
    "age": 18
}
```

The server can then create a new user.

---

# PUT

`PUT` is generally used to replace an existing resource.

Example:

```http
PUT /users/10
```

Request body:

```json
{
    "name": "Nikitha",
    "age": 19
}
```

---

# PATCH

`PATCH` is used when you only want to update part of a resource.

For example:

```http
PATCH /users/10
```

```json
{
    "age": 19
}
```

Only the age needs to change.

---

# DELETE

`DELETE` is used to remove a resource.

```http
DELETE /users/10
```

This means:

> Delete the user with ID 10.

---

# HTTP Response

After receiving a request, the server sends a response.

A response generally contains:

```text
Response
├── Status Code
├── Headers
└── Body
```

Example:

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
```

```json
{
    "name": "Nikitha"
}
```

---

# HTTP Status Codes

Status codes tell us what happened with the request.

They are divided into categories.

```text
1xx → Informational
2xx → Success
3xx → Redirection
4xx → Client Error
5xx → Server Error
```

---

# Common 2xx Codes

### 200 — OK

The request was successful.

```text
200 OK
```

Example:

A GET request successfully retrieves user information.

---

### 201 — Created

Something was successfully created.

```text
201 Created
```

Commonly returned after a successful POST request.

---

### 204 — No Content

The request was successful, but there is no response body.

Often used after deleting something.

---

# Common 3xx Codes

### 301 — Moved Permanently

The resource has permanently moved to another URL.

### 302 — Found

The resource is temporarily available at another location.

Redirects are commonly associated with 3xx responses.

---

# Common 4xx Codes

These usually indicate a problem with the request from the client side.

### 400 — Bad Request

The server cannot understand or process the request.

```text
400 Bad Request
```

---

### 401 — Unauthorized

Authentication is required or the provided authentication is invalid.

```text
401 Unauthorized
```

---

### 403 — Forbidden

The server understood the request but refuses to allow it.

```text
403 Forbidden
```

---

### 404 — Not Found

The requested resource doesn't exist.

```text
404 Not Found
```

Example:

```text
GET /users/999999
```

If that user doesn't exist, the server might return 404.

---

# Common 5xx Codes

These indicate a problem on the server side.

### 500 — Internal Server Error

Something went wrong while processing the request.

```text
500 Internal Server Error
```

### 502 — Bad Gateway

A server acting as a gateway received an invalid response from another server.

### 503 — Service Unavailable

The server is temporarily unable to handle the request.

---

# HTTP Headers

Headers provide additional information about a request or response.

Example:

```http
Content-Type: application/json
```

This tells us that the data is JSON.

Other common headers include:

```text
Authorization
Content-Type
Accept
User-Agent
Cookie
Cache-Control
```

---

# Content-Type

`Content-Type` tells the server what type of data is being sent.

For JSON:

```http
Content-Type: application/json
```

For HTML:

```http
Content-Type: text/html
```

For plain text:

```http
Content-Type: text/plain
```

---

# Request Body

The request body contains data sent to the server.

For example:

```http
POST /users
Content-Type: application/json
```

Body:

```json
{
    "name": "Nikitha",
    "email": "nikitha@example.com"
}
```

GET requests generally don't use a request body in normal web usage.

---

# URL Structure

Consider:

```text
https://example.com/products?id=10
```

It contains several parts:

```text
https://
   ↓
Protocol

example.com
   ↓
Domain

/products
   ↓
Path

?id=10
   ↓
Query Parameter
```

---

# Query Parameters

Query parameters provide additional information in a URL.

Example:

```text
/products?category=shoes
```

Another example:

```text
/products?category=shoes&sort=price
```

Here:

```text
category = shoes
sort     = price
```

Query parameters are commonly used for:

* Searching
* Filtering
* Sorting
* Pagination

---

# HTTP and APIs

HTTP is one of the main foundations of web APIs.

For example, a frontend application might request:

```http
GET /api/products
```

The server could respond:

```json
[
    {
        "id": 1,
        "name": "Laptop",
        "price": 50000
    },
    {
        "id": 2,
        "name": "Phone",
        "price": 30000
    }
]
```

The frontend can then use this data to display products.

