# Using `fetch()` in JavaScript

JavaScript provides the `fetch()` API for making HTTP requests.

Example:

```javascript
fetch("https://api.example.com/users")
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log(data);
    })
    .catch(error => {
        console.log(error);
    });
```

The basic flow is:

```text
JavaScript
    ↓
fetch()
    ↓
HTTP Request
    ↓
Server
    ↓
HTTP Response
    ↓
JSON
    ↓
JavaScript
```

---

# POST Request Using `fetch()`

```javascript
fetch("https://api.example.com/users", {

    method: "POST",

    headers: {
        "Content-Type": "application/json"
    },

    body: JSON.stringify({
        name: "Nikitha",
        age: 18
    })

})
.then(response => response.json())
.then(data => console.log(data));
```

Here:

```text
method
→ POST

headers
→ tells server we're sending JSON

body
→ actual data
```

